In article <2ea7mh$8es@agate.berkeley.edu> kingunix@garnet.berkeley.edu (Rob Broekhuis) writes:

>What does a Couatl look like, anyway?  How do they grab you?  Where does the
>name come from?  Always in a quest for trivia,

A winged snake.  Derived from South American mythology (Maian/Inca).  The name
Quetzlcouatl for instance... which was the god that they took the spanish to
be because of prophecies about his eventual return.  I'd assume they grab you
much like a constrictor snake would.  The wings are feathered and high up on
the body, leaving plenty of 'tail' free to wrap around someone.

Any other myths/beasties people wonder about?  BTW, someone told me they read
here that Elbereth was an elf.  This is false.  Elbereth was the name that the
elves (in JRR Tolkien's Middle Earth stories) gave to Varda... the queen of
Valinor / Vala / a goddess.  Among other things she was responsible for making
the stars.
